# Step 28, the management of extensions (the first half of the third pull
# request): `cineris ext list|inspect|disable|remove|add <path>`, the read
# through handles that follow no link, the way out naming the commands, and
# `extensions/status`. Each rule broken on purpose, with the tests that must
# fail. Written with the code (docs/steps/28/answered.md, point 10).

[break] unfollowed-opens-follow-a-link
rule: A folder read for `cineris ext add` follows no link at any name: every open relative to a folder's descriptor, and the folder's own, has `O_NOFOLLOW`.
os: linux
file: crates/cineris-sandbox/src/unfollowed.rs
find: const UNFOLLOWED: i32 = libc::O_NOFOLLOW | libc::O_NONBLOCK
replace: const UNFOLLOWED: i32 = libc::O_NONBLOCK
caught-by:
    cineris-sandbox unfollowed::tests::a_link_at_any_name_is_refused_and_never_followed

[break] unfollowed-takes-the-parent-as-a-name
rule: A name opened relative to a folder is one name: `..` is refused before the system is asked, so nothing above the folder is reached through its handle.
file: crates/cineris-sandbox/src/unfollowed.rs
in: one_name
find: || bytes == b".."
replace: || false
caught-by:
    cineris-sandbox unfollowed::tests::a_folder_is_read_one_name_at_a_time_through_its_handle

[break] snapshot-passes-over-a-link
rule: A link anywhere in a folder read through handles refuses the extension, never passed over: what is shown, written and pinned is the folder whole.
file: crates/cineris-ext/src/folder.rs
in: read_unfollowed_into
find: Kind::Link => return Err(FolderError::Link(relative)),
replace: Kind::Link => continue,
caught-by:
    cineris-ext folder::tests::the_read_through_handles_is_the_plain_reads_and_follows_no_link
    cineris-cli::bin/cineris ext::tests::what_cannot_be_added_or_removed_is_refused_first

